<section class="about-hero">
  <div class="about-hero__content">
    <button class="about-hero__back" type="button" onclick="history.back()">← Go back</button>
    <h1 class="about-hero__title">What are we doing?</h1>
    <p class="about-hero__lead">GreenNest explores how green and public spaces shape healthier, quieter, and more resilient cities. Urban nature plays a crucial role in improving air quality, reducing noise, cooling down heat-island zones, and supporting biodiversity. At the same time, these spaces offer calm environments where people can slow down, reflect, and mentally recharge. Using Lisbon as our main focus—and comparing it to cities with stronger green infrastructures—we map existing parks, gardens, and natural areas to understand their quality, accessibility, and their impact on both people and the ecosystems we share the city with.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="about-hero__visual" aria-hidden="true">
    <img src="/images/herillustration.svg" alt="Illustration" />
  </div>
</section>

<!-- NYT: to tekst-kolonner under hero -->
<section class="about-columns">
  <div class="col">
    <h1>How are we doing it?</h1>
    <p>We combine data, design, and environmental insights to propose how urban green spaces can evolve. First, we collect detailed information about parks and natural areas—size, vegetation, shade, seating, noise levels, and overall atmosphere—together with stress and density data. Then we visualize this through an interactive calmness map, helping users find the type of environment they need, whether quiet, shaded, social, or biodiverse. Finally, we imagine development tools and simulations that allow both citizens and planners to explore improvements—planting trees, adding water features, or increasing biodiversity—to create greener, quieter, and more restorative urban spaces for the future.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="col">
    <h1>Why are we doing it?</h1>
    <p>As cities continue to grow and urban life becomes more intense, fewer people have meaningful contact with nature in their everyday routines. This disconnect affects mental health, stress levels, social well-being, and even long-term resilience to climate change. Green spaces create a noticeable contrast in how we feel: the difference between hot asphalt and sunlit grass, between traffic noise and birdsong, is more than environmental—it’s emotional. Our project addresses the fact that 73% of Lisbon’s residents rarely use the city’s green areas, and aims to highlight why these spaces are essential for healthier cities and healthier minds.</p>
  </div>
</section>

